 <entry>
  <id>yt:video:g0mn88zpdp8</id>
  <yt:videoId>g0mn88zpdp8</yt:videoId>
  <yt:channelId>UCQ5kqUO7Pkc7YWRNnjetfbw</yt:channelId>
  <title>King Tides + Kelvin Wave hits Laguna Beach</title>
  <link rel="alternate" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g0mn88zpdp8"/>
  <author>
   <name>Skid Kids</name>
   <uri>https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCQ5kqUO7Pkc7YWRNnjetfbw</uri>
  </author>
  <published>2026-10-26T14:04:07+00:00</published>
  <updated>2026-10-30T10:08:17+00:00</updated>
  <media:group>
   <media:title>King Tides + Kelvin Wave hits Laguna Beach</media:title>
   <media:content url="https://www.youtube.com/v/g0mn88zpdp8?version=3" type="application/x-shockwave-flash" width="640" height="390"/>
   <media:thumbnail url="https://i4.ytimg.com/vi/g0mn88zpdp8/hqdefault.jpg" width="480" height="360"/>
   <media:description>Extreme high tides are to continue through the 28th and this is just the beginning.  Tide gauges off of the Scripps pier in San Diego are reading 12 to 18 inches higher than the predicted tides because of the Kelvin Wave.  It is very recognizable to see a foot more of water along our coast.  Waves are washing in to houses with a lot more power than they normally would.  While most of us are used to king tides, or large fluctuations in the tides during the winter months, what we are not used to is an additional foot and a half of water on top of a large tide fluctuation.  Kelvin waves and king tides are only part of the equation.  Our beaches have been battered by South Swell all summer long and have been left without any sand barrier.  what you see in the video is a perfect storm of all those conditions combining.</media:description>
   <media:community>
    <media:starRating count="14521" average="5.00" min="1" max="5"/>
    <media:statistics views="464092"/>
   </media:community>
  </media:group>
